The JIT memory manager reserves one buffer for the code, rodata and rwdata
sections, each padded up to the maximum section alignment, and RuntimeDyld
additionally over-reserves space for symbol stubs that are never emitted (the
generated LLVM is closed).  The whole reserved region -- padding included -- is
then copied verbatim into the heap-allocated code object, so every code object
carries a tail of whatever `new uint8_t[]` happened to hand back.  That garbage
ends up in the .cm binfiles and heap images (and in the PIDs hashed over them),
which makes builds unreproducible.

Zero the buffer on allocation.

Reported upstream: https://github.com/smlnj/smlnj/issues

--- a/smlnj/lib-codegen/object-file.cpp
+++ b/smlnj/lib-codegen/object-file.cpp
@@ -203,8 +203,11 @@
     // pad the size of the allocation so that we can guarantee correct alignment
     auto totalSize = this->size() + this->_baseAlign - 1;

-    /// allocate the memory for the in-memory sections
-    this->_mem = new uint8_t [totalSize];
+    /// allocate the memory for the in-memory sections.  The allocation is
+    /// zero initialized, because the reserved space includes inter-section
+    /// alignment padding and stub space that the JIT linker may never write
+    /// to, but that is still copied into the resulting code object.
+    this->_mem = new uint8_t [totalSize] ();
     this->_base =
         reinterpret_cast<uint8_t *>(
             alignBy(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(this->_mem), this->_baseAlign));
